    "explanation": "The provided evidence (pmid-12727991) exclusively discusses RAS mutations and PAX8-PPAR gamma rearrangements in thyroid tumors (follicular carcinoma and adenomas). It contains no information regarding BRAF V600 mutations, malignant mesothelioma, or the therapies Dabrafenib and Trametinib. Therefore, the evidence is insufficient to support, refute, or revise the claim.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-12727991",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 598,
        "quote": "\"RAS point mutations and PAX8-PPAR gamma rearrangement in thyroid tumors: evidence for distinct molecular pathways in thyroid follicular carcinoma.\\nA series of 88 conventional follicular and Hurthle cell thyroid tumors were analyzed for RAS mutations and PAX8-PPAR gamma rearrangements using molecular methods and for galectin-3 and HBME-1 expression by immunohistochemistry. A novel LightCycler technology-based method was developed to detect point mutations in codons 12/13 and 61 of the H-RAS, K-RAS, and N-RAS genes. Forty-nine percent of conventional follicular carcinomas had RAS mutations, "
      },
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-12727991",
        "start": 598,
        "end": 1003,
        "quote": "36% had PAX8-PPAR gamma rearrangement, and only one (3%) had both. In follicular adenomas, 48% had RAS mutations, 4% had PAX8-PPAR gamma rearrangement, and 48% had neither. Follicular carcinomas with PAX8-PPAR gamma typically showed immunoreactivity for galectin-3 but not for HBME-1, tended to present at a younger patient age and be smaller size, and were almost always overtly invasive. In contrast, f\""
      }
    ],
